00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:21.050
With Google Cloud
Storage, you have access

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:24.590
to a planet-scale blob storage
system with a simple interface.

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:26.990
When moving your company's
data into the cloud,

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:29.390
security is of
utmost importance.

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:31.790
That's why it's critical
to understand and enable

00:00:31.790 --> 00:00:34.220
key security systems
inside Google Cloud Storage

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:36.680
to make sure things stay safe.

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:39.560
Let's talk about some things
that Google Cloud Storage does

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:41.780
to keep your data
secure by design.

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:43.880
First, our private network--

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:47.210
users' data enters and leaves
Google's private network

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:49.320
at a point of presence
closest to them.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:51.800
This results in better
performance, cost savings,

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:54.710
and security because data is
traversing Google's network

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:57.530
with encryption in flight and
has the least possible exposure

00:00:57.530 --> 00:00:59.030
to public internet.

00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:02.510
Second, encryption is supported
as a first-class citizen.

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:04.489
Data in Cloud Storage
is always encrypted

00:01:04.489 --> 00:01:07.820
at rest with several layers
and in flight via SSL/TLS

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:10.040
by default. This is no choice.

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:12.620
On top, we allow you to
supply your own encryption

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:15.660
keys as well just in case
you need more control.

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:18.600
Third, we support
comprehensive auditing.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:21.870
This is especially important
in highly regulated industries.

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:24.140
Things like cloud audit
logs, access to storage

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:27.060
logs, and Stackdriver monitoring
can help your application

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:29.600
with any compliance
[? stance ?] you may have.

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:31.850
Finally, integration
of Data Loss

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:35.660
Prevention, or short, DLP, which
allows you to redact, encrypt,

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:38.630
and desensitize personally
identifiable information

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:42.200
in your data and get a very good
grip on your data via analytics

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.340
and visualization.

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:47.070
Now Google Cloud Storage aims
to be as secure as possible.

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:49.310
But even with that, there
are some key patterns

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:51.890
that you can use to ensure
your security regulation even

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:53.750
further.

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:56.622
One is to properly separate
privileged access to your data.

00:01:56.622 --> 00:01:58.580
Sharing your data through
the right [? hoops ?]

00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:01.670
and right people is a
constant and ongoing concern.

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:04.460
As such, it's good practice
to use Google Key Management

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:07.460
Service, or short, KMS,
to define storage access

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:09.560
permissions to groups
of individuals.

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:13.160
Secondly, we use that same KMS
to help encrypt data at scale

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:14.690
for [INAUDIBLE].

00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:17.420
For example, rather than sending
files with sensitive info

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:20.000
to KMS before storing
them, which wouldn't scale,

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:22.370
files are encrypted using
a local encryption key,

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:25.520
and then simply use KMS to
manage that key over time.

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:27.740
Third, it's important to
understand the right way

00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:29.630
to delete your data
in a secure manner,

00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:31.257
and this one is a tricky one.

00:02:31.257 --> 00:02:32.840
As your data moves
through your system

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:34.850
for various types
of analysis and use,

00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:36.640
it's getting proliferated.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:38.240
As deletion comes
around, you want

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:40.400
to delete the entire
footprint, which is harder

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:43.550
to track down than you might
think and could take some time.

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:45.830
To solve this quickly,
you can once again

00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:47.540
use Key Management Service.

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:49.010
Rather than deleting
the data, you

00:02:49.010 --> 00:02:51.530
can simply delete the
key used to encrypt it,

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:53.630
which effectively renders
the data meaningless.

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:56.690
And then clean up later,
remove the unused data

00:02:56.690 --> 00:02:58.430
as a form of garbage collection.

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:01.100
The last [INAUDIBLE] is about
preventing deletion of data

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:02.870
for regulatory purposes.

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:05.060
In some scenarios,
you can revoke access

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:07.970
based on policies to
lock the data for you

00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:10.370
a certain number of
seconds or up to years

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:12.620
and then give
access back to you.

